with martial arts brawler sifu developer  sloclap is hoping to capture the meaning  of kung fu creating an experience of  learning and mastery through practice  according to slo-clap ceo and co-founder  pierre tarno that means you should  expect to die a lot as you brawl your  way through the game but as you do  you'll become older wiser and stronger  both as a player and as a character slow  clap recently gave gamespot a hands-off  look at three of sifu's five different  levels providing us with a more complete  look at what your quest for revenge will  actually play like if you enjoy this  video and you want to see more coverage  of sifu in the future make sure to hit  the like and subscribe buttons below  if you played absolver slo-clap's last  martial arts game you'll have an idea of  what sifu is like you play a kung fu  student whose family is murdered and you  set out on a one-day quest for revenge  against the five kung fu fighters  responsible to get to them you have to  battle your way through five different  levels filled with each of the boss's  fighters and each level is themed on a  different element in chinese philosophy  wood fire water earth and metal those  levels are each set in a different  location in a fictional chinese city but  tarnow told us that as you venture  deeper into each one they'll become less  and less realistic and more and more  fantastical as the theme of their  elements builds in combat sifu will  probably be pretty familiar to fans of  games with similar martial arts combat  like absolver you execute combos through  different combinations of light and  heavy attacks while also dodging  blocking and parrying your enemies moves  tarno said players should expect the  game to be pretty challenging and we got  that impression from the hands-off  presentation enemies come at you fast  and often in big groups requiring you to  learn how to deal with their moves  isolate fighters and control the crowd  you can't just block your way to victory  you have a gauge that measures your  guard strength and as you take hits or  block blows it fills up indicating when  your guard will be broken and leaving  you stunned enemies also have a similar  gauge indicating when you can bust  through their guard leaving them open  for a quick takedown that knocks them  out of the fight with a cinematic  flourish while also replenishing some of  your health  [Music]  so there's an incentive to play  aggressively especially because you can  take a beating and lose your health  pretty quickly thanks to a special  amulet that you wear being defeated  doesn't mean you die you just pop right  back up and go on brawling however your  age progresses significantly every time  you die and if you age too much on a run  it's game over we didn't see too much of  the aging mechanic in action but tarnow  told us a bit about how it works he said  the fantasy at play is the idea of  becoming a wise master becoming more  powerful and knowledgeable the older you  get but of course getting old has its  drawbacks the system is still being  solidified but the general idea is that  the older you get the better a kung fu  fighter you become so your hits will be  more powerful and land with more  precision the drawback is that getting  older takes a toll on your body slow  clap doesn't want you to become weaker  as you age because it doesn't want to  lock you into a loop where the more you  die the more likely you are to die again  until it's game over but your character  will have less health as they age so  you'll have to be more and more  aggressive as you get older taking down  enemies more quickly to keep them from  taking you down we saw three different  locations from the game an apartment  building a nightclub that houses a  secret fight club and a museum each one  presents different environments to fight  through and it's essential that you pay  attention to your surroundings because  taking advantage of the space around you  is key to staying alive you can  consistently use your environment in  combat and really it's essential in the  apartment building you fight your way up  a big central staircase which means you  can throw enemies over the railing into  space if you're close to a wall you can  bounce an enemy's head off it or throw  them into it pushing someone down a  flight of stairs or out a window helps  you to eliminate enemies quickly and  anything from empty glass bottles to  pieces of furniture can potentially be a  weapon to give you the upper hand from  the looks of things sifu's combat is all  about keeping control of the situation  or getting out of harm's way quickly at  one point in the nightclub the player  was backed into a corner and so quickly  scrambled up some decorative lattice to  a higher floor later in the nightclub  the player quickly rolled over a couch  to get some distance on some enemies who  were closing in you could also unlock  different moves that let you throw  enemies into one another swap places  with them or knock them down unlocking  these moves and working them into your  combos allows you to constantly be  thinking about how to control the fight  even when facing overwhelming odds the  same goes for when you're fighting  enemies with weapons knock an enemy down  and you disarm them so you want to be  thinking about how to get enemies off  their feet and out of the fight before  they slash you with a machete or nail  you with a baseball bat finally fighting  well builds up a gauge for your  character called focus when you've got  enough focus you can briefly slow down  time allowing you to pick from a group  of powerful moves that have special  effects one focus move might leave an  enemy stunned while another might knock  them down or do extra damage you can  also unlock moves specific to the  weapons you can find and use like blades  to deal massive damage under the right  circumstances it seems like sifu will  have a whole lot of options for fighting  and it might be a lot to keep track of  tarnow said the player character has  around 150 different moves all created  with the help of a real-life pakmy kung  fu master and that doesn't count the  many takedown moves but you won't have  access to every move every time you play  the game instead it'll take you time to  unlock various different abilities and  for a while you'll only have those  abilities for the specific run you're  actually playing at that time as you  rack up combos and beat enemies you'll  earn experience points you can then  spend at certain places to unlock new  moves either shrines you find in levels  which convey something like perks that  only last through a given run of the  game or at your wu  the dojo you return to between levels  but while you can unlock a move during a  run you'll lose it if you age out and  die until you've spent enough points to  unlock it permanently that'll give you  time to figure out which abilities you  like and want to use in your kung fu and  which you don't that gave sifu a bit of  a roguelike flare although tarnow  clarified that each level and each  encounter is handcrafted they're not  random what's different each time is the  set of perks and moves you might have  unlocked especially as you fight and age  there are also elements of randomness as  you play through a given level if you  efficiently wail on a group of enemies  one might surrender allowing you to  avoid fighting them or even question  them for information another enemy in  the same situation might become the last  man standing gaining a health boost and  unlocking additional combos to become  something like a mini boss all the  enemies have access to the same moves  you do so fighting these new characters  will be a test of your knowledge and  experience and they can pop up  semi-randomly so there are rogue-like  elements at play in sifu mixing up the  play of a straightforward action game  but you'll also be unlocking things that  will persist between runs through the  game tarno described another element a  menu called the detective board where  you'll gather information about each of  the bosses and the levels as you play  through them the more info you have the  more paths you might unlock in a level  allowing you to access shortcuts or  avoid fights again the theme is mastery  through practice so you'll replay levels  over and over learning their ins and  outs and gathering info on your  detective board while also becoming a  better fighter and unlocking more and  more moves though we only saw three  brief sections of the game it seems like  all these systems will work together to  make sifu a pretty deep action  experience but one that's excitingly  cinematic the focus on paying attention  to and using the environment makes for  some great moments like one in which the  player battles a gang of enemies in a  museum exhibit with a giant kunai shaped  pendulum there are also moments where  the camera will shift perspective like a  hallway full of enemies that suddenly  adopts a side-scrolling perspective  which tarnow said was an homage to the  movie old boy and to classic 2d brawlers  expect the game to run at 60fps on both  playstation 4 and playstation 5 tarno  said and in 4k on ps5 it all makes sifu  look like an impressive if tough and  demanding action game one in which  you'll want to keep practicing to become  a kung fu master sifu is set to release  on ps4 ps5 and pc on february 22nd  [Music]  you
 

